{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interval charts answer where a span begins and ends. The endpoints may\nrepresent time, uncertainty, a daily trading range, or a percentile envelope.\nModel those meanings explicitly with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":"; do not\nforce intervals through a point-value channel."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How did each trading day move from open to close?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Horizontal price interval"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How uncertain is each point estimate?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Point plus low-high error bar"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What were open, high, low, and close for each period?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Candlestick"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does a percentile range evolve over time?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantile ribbon plus median line"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do explicit lower and upper measurements change together?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range area in "},{"type":"link","href":"./lines-and-areas.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lines and Areas"}]}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition may receive endpoint fields directly or derive them from typed\nreducer outputs. In either form, endpoint units must match the scale.\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defines these interval\nchannel shapes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-open-to-close-spans","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare open-to-close spans"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This view maps each AAPL trading date to a categorical lane and its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Open"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Close"},{"type":"text","value":" fields to a horizontal rectangle. Color distinguishes gains from\nlosses, while the endpoints carry the price movement directly."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"13-interval-timeline","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep trading dates stable and lane order explicit. Date labels rely on automatic\nguide measurement; verify them at the smallest supported width with\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"preserve-uncertainty-bounds","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve uncertainty bounds"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An error bar combines a point estimate, a low-high link, and endpoint ticks.\nThe chart renders the supplied interval; it does not decide whether the bounds\nare standard deviation, standard error, a confidence interval, or a credible\ninterval."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"error-bar-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"error-bar","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart, dot, link, tickY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { estimates } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    link(estimates, {\\n      x1: 'treatment',\\n      y1: 'low',\\n      x2: 'treatment',\\n      y2: 'high',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n    tickY(estimates, {\\n      x: 'treatment',\\n      y: 'low',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n    tickY(estimates, {\\n      x: 'treatment',\\n      y: 'high',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n    dot(estimates, {\\n      x: 'treatment',\\n      y: 'estimate',\\n      key: 'treatment',\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n      r: 4,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.3) },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Mean response (95% confidence interval)' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const estimates = [\\n  { treatment: 'Control', estimate: 42, low: 36, high: 48 },\\n  { treatment: 'Low dose', estimate: 51, low: 45, high: 57 },\\n  { treatment: 'High dose', estimate: 63, low: 56, high: 70 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart, dot, link, tickY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { estimates } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(estimates, {\n      x1: 'treatment',\n      y1: 'low',\n      x2: 'treatment',\n      y2: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'estimate',\n      key: 'treatment',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.3) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Mean response (95% confidence interval)' },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=error-bar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const estimates = [\n  { treatment: 'Control', estimate: 42, low: 36, high: 48 },\n  { treatment: 'Low dose', estimate: 51, low: 45, high: 57 },\n  { treatment: 'High dose', estimate: 63, low: 56, high: 70 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=error-bar file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Name the interval in the chart description or surrounding prose. Compose the\nlink, caps, and point as separate layers using the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dot and Hexagon Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/14-error-bars/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full catalog example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nstarts with contributing observations, groups them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":", and\nderives the interval from typed mean and sample-deviation outputs. The\nestimator and singleton policy remain authored chart meaning; no dedicated\nerror-bar mark is required."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"encode-open-high-low-and-close","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Encode open, high, low, and close"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A candlestick uses a high-low wick and an open-close body. Directional color is\nsecondary to the body endpoints and should not be the only way to distinguish\nan increasing period from a decreasing one."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"28-candlestick","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use one row per period with all four values. Render the wick as a link and the\nbody as a ranged rectangle; preserve missing trading periods on the temporal\ndomain instead of silently inventing observations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"show-an-interval-over-time","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show an interval over time"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A quantile ribbon combines a prepared lower percentile, median, and upper\npercentile for each time group. It shows how both location and spread evolve."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"61-quantile-ribbon","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"quantile"},{"type":"text","value":" reducers to preserve each time group's source\nrows, then give the ribbon and median their own marks. "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms and\nReactivity"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defines the aggregation\nboundary; "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/line-and-area.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line and Area Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defines the\nrange-area channels."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State what each endpoint means and whether the interval is inclusive."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use timezones and calendar boundaries intentionally for temporal spans."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep interval semantics in data fields rather than inferring them from color\nor row order."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve exact values through a tooltip, table, or textual summary. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use semantic controls and application state when intervals become editable;\nsee "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify essential distinctions without color in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rectangle channel details are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar and Rect Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"compare-open-to-close-spans","text":"Compare open-to-close spans","level":2},{"id":"preserve-uncertainty-bounds","text":"Preserve uncertainty bounds","level":2},{"id":"encode-open-high-low-and-close","text":"Encode open, high, low, and close","level":2},{"id":"show-an-interval-over-time","text":"Show an interval over time","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nInterval charts answer where a span begins and ends. The endpoints may\nrepresent time, uncertainty, a daily trading range, or a percentile envelope.\nModel those meanings explicitly with `x1` and `x2` or `y1` and `y2`; do not\nforce intervals through a point-value channel.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                               | Start with                                            |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| How did each trading day move from open to close?             | Horizontal price interval                             |\n| How uncertain is each point estimate?                         | Point plus low-high error bar                         |\n| What were open, high, low, and close for each period?         | Candlestick                                           |\n| How does a percentile range evolve over time?                 | Quantile ribbon plus median line                      |\n| How do explicit lower and upper measurements change together? | Range area in [Lines and Areas](./lines-and-areas.md) |\n\nThe definition may receive endpoint fields directly or derive them from typed\nreducer outputs. In either form, endpoint units must match the scale.\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) defines these interval\nchannel shapes.\n\n## Compare open-to-close spans\n\nThis view maps each AAPL trading date to a categorical lane and its `Open` and\n`Close` fields to a horizontal rectangle. Color distinguishes gains from\nlosses, while the endpoints carry the price movement directly.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=13-interval-timeline height=480 -->\n\nKeep trading dates stable and lane order explicit. Date labels rely on automatic\nguide measurement; verify them at the smallest supported width with\n[Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n\n## Preserve uncertainty bounds\n\nAn error bar combines a point estimate, a low-high link, and endpoint ticks.\nThe chart renders the supplied interval; it does not decide whether the bounds\nare standard deviation, standard error, a confidence interval, or a credible\ninterval.\n\n```ts group=error-bar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, tickY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { estimates } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(estimates, {\n      x1: 'treatment',\n      y1: 'low',\n      x2: 'treatment',\n      y2: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'estimate',\n      key: 'treatment',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.3) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Mean response (95% confidence interval)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=error-bar file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const estimates = [\n  { treatment: 'Control', estimate: 42, low: 36, high: 48 },\n  { treatment: 'Low dose', estimate: 51, low: 45, high: 57 },\n  { treatment: 'High dose', estimate: 63, low: 56, high: 70 },\n]\n```\n\nName the interval in the chart description or surrounding prose. Compose the\nlink, caps, and point as separate layers using the\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\nand [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n\nThe [full catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/14-error-bars/)\nstarts with contributing observations, groups them once with `groupBy`, and\nderives the interval from typed mean and sample-deviation outputs. The\nestimator and singleton policy remain authored chart meaning; no dedicated\nerror-bar mark is required.\n\n## Encode open, high, low, and close\n\nA candlestick uses a high-low wick and an open-close body. Directional color is\nsecondary to the body endpoints and should not be the only way to distinguish\nan increasing period from a decreasing one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=28-candlestick height=480 -->\n\nUse one row per period with all four values. Render the wick as a link and the\nbody as a ranged rectangle; preserve missing trading periods on the temporal\ndomain instead of silently inventing observations.\n\n## Show an interval over time\n\nA quantile ribbon combines a prepared lower percentile, median, and upper\npercentile for each time group. It shows how both location and spread evolve.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=61-quantile-ribbon height=480 -->\n\nUse `groupBy` with `quantile` reducers to preserve each time group's source\nrows, then give the ribbon and median their own marks. [Transforms and\nReactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) defines the aggregation\nboundary; [Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md) defines the\nrange-area channels.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- State what each endpoint means and whether the interval is inclusive.\n- Use timezones and calendar boundaries intentionally for temporal spans.\n- Keep interval semantics in data fields rather than inferring them from color\n  or row order.\n- Preserve exact values through a tooltip, table, or textual summary. See\n  [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n- Use semantic controls and application state when intervals become editable;\n  see [Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md).\n- Verify essential distinctions without color in\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n\nRectangle channel details are in\n[Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Intervals and Financial Charts\ndescription: Compose timelines, uncertainty intervals, candlesticks, and percentile ribbons from explicit endpoints.\n---\n\nInterval charts answer where a span begins and ends. The endpoints may\nrepresent time, uncertainty, a daily trading range, or a percentile envelope.\nModel those meanings explicitly with `x1` and `x2` or `y1` and `y2`; do not\nforce intervals through a point-value channel.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                               | Start with                                            |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| How did each trading day move from open to close?             | Horizontal price interval                             |\n| How uncertain is each point estimate?                         | Point plus low-high error bar                         |\n| What were open, high, low, and close for each period?         | Candlestick                                           |\n| How does a percentile range evolve over time?                 | Quantile ribbon plus median line                      |\n| How do explicit lower and upper measurements change together? | Range area in [Lines and Areas](./lines-and-areas.md) |\n\nThe definition may receive endpoint fields directly or derive them from typed\nreducer outputs. In either form, endpoint units must match the scale.\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) defines these interval\nchannel shapes.\n\n## Compare open-to-close spans\n\nThis view maps each AAPL trading date to a categorical lane and its `Open` and\n`Close` fields to a horizontal rectangle. Color distinguishes gains from\nlosses, while the endpoints carry the price movement directly.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=13-interval-timeline height=480 -->\n\nKeep trading dates stable and lane order explicit. Date labels rely on automatic\nguide measurement; verify them at the smallest supported width with\n[Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n\n## Preserve uncertainty bounds\n\nAn error bar combines a point estimate, a low-high link, and endpoint ticks.\nThe chart renders the supplied interval; it does not decide whether the bounds\nare standard deviation, standard error, a confidence interval, or a credible\ninterval.\n\n```ts group=error-bar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart, dot, link, tickY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { estimates } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    link(estimates, {\n      x1: 'treatment',\n      y1: 'low',\n      x2: 'treatment',\n      y2: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'low',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    tickY(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'high',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n    dot(estimates, {\n      x: 'treatment',\n      y: 'estimate',\n      key: 'treatment',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      r: 4,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.3) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Mean response (95% confidence interval)' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=error-bar file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const estimates = [\n  { treatment: 'Control', estimate: 42, low: 36, high: 48 },\n  { treatment: 'Low dose', estimate: 51, low: 45, high: 57 },\n  { treatment: 'High dose', estimate: 63, low: 56, high: 70 },\n]\n```\n\nName the interval in the chart description or surrounding prose. Compose the\nlink, caps, and point as separate layers using the\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks reference](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md)\nand [Dot and Hexagon Marks](../reference/marks/dot-and-hexagon.md).\n\nThe [full catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/14-error-bars/)\nstarts with contributing observations, groups them once with `groupBy`, and\nderives the interval from typed mean and sample-deviation outputs. The\nestimator and singleton policy remain authored chart meaning; no dedicated\nerror-bar mark is required.\n\n## Encode open, high, low, and close\n\nA candlestick uses a high-low wick and an open-close body. Directional color is\nsecondary to the body endpoints and should not be the only way to distinguish\nan increasing period from a decreasing one.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=28-candlestick height=480 -->\n\nUse one row per period with all four values. Render the wick as a link and the\nbody as a ranged rectangle; preserve missing trading periods on the temporal\ndomain instead of silently inventing observations.\n\n## Show an interval over time\n\nA quantile ribbon combines a prepared lower percentile, median, and upper\npercentile for each time group. It shows how both location and spread evolve.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=61-quantile-ribbon height=480 -->\n\nUse `groupBy` with `quantile` reducers to preserve each time group's source\nrows, then give the ribbon and median their own marks. [Transforms and\nReactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) defines the aggregation\nboundary; [Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md) defines the\nrange-area channels.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- State what each endpoint means and whether the interval is inclusive.\n- Use timezones and calendar boundaries intentionally for temporal spans.\n- Keep interval semantics in data fields rather than inferring them from color\n  or row order.\n- Preserve exact values through a tooltip, table, or textual summary. See\n  [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md).\n- Use semantic controls and application state when intervals become editable;\n  see [Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md).\n- Verify essential distinctions without color in\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n\nRectangle channel details are in\n[Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md).\n"}
